Here's the playbook, based on what actually worked in this project. Three stages, each with a copy-paste prompt.

1. Create the plan

Read the codebase first. I want to [GOAL â€” e.g. "add X feature"].
Write two documents in _dev/upgrade/:

1. proposition-final.md â€” the authoritative spec: objective, architectural
   decisions, what must be preserved, data schemas, API surface, security
   rules, and a definition of done.

2. implementation-plan.md â€” break the spec into phases and commit-sized
   steps. STRICT FORMAT (the loop orchestrator parses it):
   - phases as:  ## Phase N â€” Title
   - steps as:   ### N.M Title
   - every step ends with:  **Done when:** <verifiable criteria>
   Phase 0 must be audit/contracts (verify the plan against the real code
   before building). Each step must leave the app working.

Ground every claim in the actual code â€” read the files, don't assume.
The format rules matter: _dev/loop-engineering/loop_engineering.py parses exactly those headings and **Done when:** lines, and detects progress from commit subjects containing step N.M.

2. Gate the plan before building

Review _dev/upgrade/implementation-plan.md against proposition-final.md and
the real repository. Check every step's inputs/outputs against actual code,
resolve contradictions in favor of working behavior, freeze the machine
contracts (schemas, API shapes, error codes) in contracts.md, and record a
gate: what's complete, what's deferred with an owner, what blocks Phase 1.
Do not start implementation.
This is the round that caught the studio/scenes-doesn't-exist class of problems here â€” cheapest bugs you'll ever fix.

3. Run it
Hands-free (the orchestrator):


_dev\loop-engineering\run.bat --status            :: see progress + next step
_dev\loop-engineering\run.bat --dry-run --phase 2 :: preview
_dev\loop-engineering\run.bat --phase 2           :: execute until phase 2 done
It builds each step with Claude, validates with pytest/vitest/build itself, fix-loops while red, has codex review the commits, and halts for you only when stuck. For a new project, point PLAN_PATH at the new plan (or copy the folder) and seed state with --mark-done-through / --sync-git.

Interactive (what you did with me): just say


execute step 2.4 then commit
Naming the step beats "next phase" â€” it's one commit-sized unit, unambiguous, and the commit subject (step 2.4) keeps the orchestrator's state in sync automatically. Periodically interleave:


review the new implementation, find bugs   â† adversarial pass, report only
fix the critical and high findings, then commit
The one-liner that ties it together for a brand-new goal:


Plan first: transform [GOAL] into _dev/upgrade/implementation-plan.md using
the phase/step/Done-when format the loop orchestrator parses, gate it
against the real code, then execute it step by step â€” validate and commit
after each step.
Two habits worth keeping from this project: never let a step commit on a red board (say "tests must be green before committing" if running headless), and one writer at a time â€” don't run the loop while an interactive session is editing the same repo.
